.\" mariadb-plugin
.SH "NAME"
mariadb-plugin \- search for, install and uninstall MariaDB server plugins
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.HP \w'\fBmariadb-plugin\ [\fR\fB\fIoptions\fR\fR\fB]\ search\ [\fR\fB\fIplugin\fR\fR\fB]\fR\ 'u
\fBmariadb-plugin [\fR\fB\fIoptions\fR\fR\fB] search [\fR\fB\fIplugin\fR\fR\fB]\fR
.HP \w'\fBmariadb-plugin\ [\fR\fB\fIoptions\fR\fR\fB]\ install\ \fR\fB\fIplugin\fR\fR\ 'u
\fBmariadb-plugin [\fR\fB\fIoptions\fR\fR\fB] install \fR\fB\fIplugin\fR\fR
.HP \w'\fBmariadb-plugin\ [\fR\fB\fIoptions\fR\fR\fB]\ uninstall\ \fR\fB\fIplugin\fR\fR\ 'u
\fBmariadb-plugin [\fR\fB\fIoptions\fR\fR\fB] uninstall \fR\fB\fIplugin\fR\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
The
\fBmariadb-plugin\fR
utility installs, uninstalls and searches for MariaDB server plugins\&.
.PP
Plugins are named the same way on every platform,
\fIplugin\fR
being the plugin name without any prefix, for instance
rocksdb
or
provider\-lz4\&. The tool builds the package name from it, so that
rocksdb
becomes
mariadb\-plugin\-rocksdb\&.
.PP
The build layout selects how
\fBmariadb-plugin\fR
installs a plugin. At runtime it validates that its own executable belongs to
the matching installation. On RPM or DEB installations the work is left to
the system package manager, so that the plugin
files stay owned by it and dependency resolution, signature checks and
upgrades keep working\&.
\fBapt\-get\fR
is used on DEB installations, and
\fBdnf\fR
or
\fBzypper\fR
on RPM ones, whichever is present\&. The package manager talks to the user
directly, asks for confirmation itself, and its exit status is passed
through unchanged\&. Installing and uninstalling this way requires root\&.
.PP
On RPM installations the packages carry their own names, such as
MariaDB\-rocksdb\-engine, and answer to the uniform name through
Provides\&. Uninstall resolves the real package name before removing it\&.
.PP
On binary tarball installations, install reads a local archive specified by
\fB\-\-file\fR, or downloads one from the plugin repository\&. Downloads
require a matching SHA\-256 checksum from the repository index\&. The tool
validates the archive, refuses to overwrite files, records installed paths
under \fI<basedir>/.mariadb\-plugin/\fR, and prints instructions for enabling
the plugin\&. It does not edit server configuration\&. Uninstall uses the
recorded paths and does not need network access\&. Before removing files,
uninstall validates the manifest's plugin name and paths. An invalid,
unreadable or non-regular manifest causes no deletion. If a file cannot be
removed, the manifest is kept so uninstall can be retried\&.
.PP
The deprecated
ENABLE/DISABLE
syntax requires the legacy plugin configuration file and an already-installed
plugin library. It does not extract an archive. Use the activation instructions
printed after a tarball installation, or the server's
INSTALL SONAME
statement or
\fB\-\-plugin\-load\-add\fR
option\&.
.PP
An error is given if
\fBmariadb-plugin\fR
does not run from the installation it belongs to, for example when it is
started from a build directory, as it would otherwise act on an unrelated
installation\&.
.PP
To install a plugin:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
shell> \fBmariadb-plugin install rocksdb\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.PP
To see which plugins are available and which are already installed, with an
optional word to search for:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
shell> \fBmariadb-plugin search\fR
shell> \fBmariadb-plugin search provider\fR
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.PP
Each plugin is listed with its name, whether it is installed or only
available, and its description\&. Searching needs no root, and ends with a
non\-zero status when nothing matches\&.
.PP
On tarball installations, search reads the same repository index as install,
lists compatible builds, and filters by a substring of the plugin name\&.
It downloads only metadata and does not change installation files\&.
The installed status means that this tool has a readable manifest with a
matching plugin name and valid path records\&. It does not verify file health
or whether the server has loaded the plugin; an interrupted installation can
also leave a manifest\&. Invalid or unreadable manifests cause an error\&.
Bundled libraries without a manifest are shown as available when listed in
the repository\&. Plugins absent from the index are omitted from search but
can still be uninstalled using their local manifests\&.
.PP
Windows ZIP packages are not implemented yet\&.
.SH "TARBALL REPOSITORIES"
.PP
\fB\-\-base\-url\fR specifies an HTTP or HTTPS directory containing
\fBplugins.index\fR and the plugin archives\&. No default repository is
configured yet, so tarball search and downloading require this option\&. HTTPS is
recommended; certificate verification is enabled\&. An HTTPS download
cannot redirect to HTTP\&.
.PP
The index is a restricted text format, not a general YAML document\&.
Each line is \fIkey: value\fR, with a blank line between plugin records\&.
Lines, including their newline, must fit in 1024 bytes\&. For example:
.sp
.nf
name: example
version: 1.0
server: 13.1
platform: Linux
arch: x86_64
file: mariadb\-plugin\-example\-1.0.tar.gz
sha256: <64 hexadecimal characters>
.fi
.PP
All seven fields are required\&. The server major/minor version, platform
and architecture must match the build of \fBmariadb-plugin\fR\&. The platform
and architecture are shown by \fB\-\-version\fR\&. The tool refuses an index
with multiple matching records rather than guessing which version to use\&.
The optional author, description and license fields are accepted\&. Search
prints the description, or leaves that column empty when it is missing\&.
Duplicate occurrences of these fields are rejected, as are duplicate required
fields\&. Other fields are ignored\&. Values are literal text, without YAML
quoting or escaping\&. The final metadata format remains under development\&.
.PP
Archive filenames must end in \fB.tar.gz\fR and contain only letters,
digits, dots, underscores and hyphens, without consecutive dots\&.
They cannot contain directory paths or URLs\&. Keep the original CPack
filename so the installer can recognize the archive's wrapper directory\&.
The repository index is limited to 8 MiB and each downloaded archive to 1 GiB\&.
.SH "DEPRECATED SYNTAX"
.PP
The former syntax
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
mariadb-plugin [\fIoptions\fR] \fIplugin\fR {ENABLE|DISABLE}
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.PP
is deprecated and kept only for backward compatibility\&. It does not
install or remove any files: it enables or disables plugins in the
mysql\&.plugin
table by starting the server in bootstrap mode, which means the server must
not already be running\&. The plugins to configure are read from a
configuration file named after the plugin library, for instance
myplugins\&.ini
for
myplugins\&.so, listing the library name on the first line and the
components to enable on the following ones\&.
.PP
The
\fB\-\-basedir\fR,
\fB\-\-datadir\fR,
\fB\-\-plugin\-dir\fR,
\fB\-\-plugin\-ini\fR,
\fB\-\-mysqld\fR,
\fB\-\-my\-print\-defaults\fR
and
\fB\-\-lc\-messages\-dir\fR
options apply to this syntax only\&.
.PP
For new setups, use
INSTALL SONAME
or the
\fB\-\-plugin\-load\-add\fR
server option instead\&.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
mariadb-plugin Options
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: help option
.\" help option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-help\fR,
\fB\-?\fR
.sp
Display a help message and exit\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: dry-run option
.\" dry-run option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
.sp
Print the commands that
install
and
uninstall
would run, without running them\&. The package name is resolved as usual, so
the command shown is the one that would be executed\&. Needs no root\&.
For tarball downloads, fetch the index and show the chosen URL and checksum,
without downloading the archive or changing the installation\&. For a local
archive, validate it and print the planned extraction\&.
This option is rejected with the deprecated ENABLE/DISABLE syntax\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: verbose option
.\" verbose option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-verbose\fR,
\fB\-v\fR
.sp
Verbose mode\&. Print more information about what the program does\&. This
option can be used multiple times to increase the amount of information\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: version option
.\" version option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-version\fR,
\fB\-V\fR
.sp
Display version information and exit\&.
.RE
.SS "Tarball Options"
.TP
\fB\-\-base\-url=\fIURL\fR
Override the plugin repository directory\&. Applies to tarball search and install;
cannot be combined with \fB\-\-file\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-file=\fIpath\fR
Install from a local regular archive file without using the repository\&.
The archive is copied to private temporary storage before verification and
extraction, so replacing the source path cannot change the installed bytes\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-sha256=\fIhex\fR
Expected checksum of a local archive\&. Requires \fB\-\-file\fR\&.
Without this option, local installation warns that the archive is unverified\&.
Downloaded archives always require the checksum supplied in the index\&.
Search rejects \fB\-\-file\fR and \fB\-\-sha256\fR\&.
RPM and DEB installations also reject \fB\-\-base\-url\fR for search;
they use native package repositories\&. They reject all three options for
install\&.
.PP
The following options apply to the deprecated
ENABLE
and
DISABLE
syntax only\&. They can be specified on the command line or in the
[mariadbd]
group of any option file, where
\fBmariadb-plugin\fR
recognizes
\fB\-\-basedir\fR,
\fB\-\-datadir\fR,
\fB\-\-plugin\-dir\fR
and
\fB\-\-lc\-messages\-dir\fR
and ignores other options from that group\&.
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: basedir option
.\" basedir option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-basedir=\fR\fB\fIpath\fR\fR,
\fB\-b \fR\fB\fIpath\fR\fR
.sp
The server base directory\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: datadir option
.\" datadir option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-datadir=\fR\fB\fIpath\fR\fR,
\fB\-d \fR\fB\fIpath\fR\fR
.sp
The server data directory\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: plugin-dir option
.\" plugin-dir option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-plugin\-dir=\fR\fB\fIpath\fR\fR,
\fB\-p \fR\fB\fIpath\fR\fR
.sp
The server plugin directory\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: plugin-ini option
.\" plugin-ini option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-plugin\-ini=\fR\fB\fIfile_name\fR\fR,
\fB\-i \fR\fB\fIfile_name\fR\fR
.sp
The
\fBmariadb-plugin\fR
configuration file\&. Relative path names are interpreted relative to the
current directory\&. If this option is not given, the default is
\fIplugin\fR\&.ini
in the plugin directory, where
\fIplugin\fR
is the
\fIplugin\fR
argument on the command line\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: mysqld option
.\" mysqld option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-mysqld=\fR\fB\fIpath\fR\fR,
\fB\-m \fR\fB\fIpath\fR\fR
.sp
The path to the
\fBmariadbd\fR
server\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: my-print-defaults option
.\" my-print-defaults option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-my\-print\-defaults=\fR\fB\fIpath\fR\fR,
\fB\-f \fR\fB\fIpath\fR\fR
.sp
The path to the
\fBmy_print_defaults\fR
program\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: lc-messages-dir option
.\" lc-messages-dir option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-lc\-messages\-dir=\fR\fB\fIpath\fR\fR,
\fB\-l \fR\fB\fIpath\fR\fR
.sp
The directory with the server error messages\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: no-defaults option
.\" no-defaults option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-no\-defaults\fR,
\fB\-n\fR
.sp
Do not read values from the configuration file\&. This option enables an
administrator to skip reading defaults from the configuration file\&.
.sp
With
\fBmariadb-plugin\fR, this option need not be given first on the command line,
unlike most other MariaDB programs that support
\fB\-\-no\-defaults\fR\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
.\" mariadb-plugin: print-defaults option
.\" print-defaults option: mariadb-plugin
\fB\-\-print\-defaults\fR,
\fB\-P\fR
.sp
Display the default values from the configuration file\&. This option causes
\fBmariadb-plugin\fR
to print the defaults for
\fB\-\-basedir\fR,
\fB\-\-datadir\fR, and
\fB\-\-plugin\-dir\fR
if they are found in the configuration file\&. If no value for a variable is
found, nothing is shown\&.
.sp
With
\fBmariadb-plugin\fR, this option need not be given first on the command line,
unlike most other MariaDB programs that support
\fB\-\-print\-defaults\fR\&.
.RE
